WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.350
What does that famous final shot of Stanley
Kubrick's The Shining really mean, and what

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.680
happens to the traumatized characters left
alive?

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.980
It's time to talk about those questions, and
dig up some answers.

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:15.140
In order to survive the events of The Shining,
Danny Torrance was forced to escape his own

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:19.740
father in a freezing hedge maze and flee a
haunted hotel where he'd seen enough freaky

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.260
stuff to seriously mess up even your average
adult.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.120
"Come and play with us, Danny."

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.740
So while he lived to tell the tale, it's tough
to imagine Danny has a normal life waiting

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:36.800
for him in the aftermath.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.210
If you want to go with Stephen King's version
of events, Danny's future is at least partially

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:41.700
written.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.020
In the 2013 novel Doctor Sleep, King's official
sequel to The Shining, a grown Danny Torrance

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.160
suffers from alcoholism like his father.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.180
Danny gets a job at a nursing home where he's
able to help guide the dying into the next

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:53.680
realm.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.560
He eventually encounters a girl with abilities
like his own and fights to save her from a

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.260
group of psychic vampires.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:00.020
Sounds strange?

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:04.480
Doctor Sleep is headed for the big screen
in late 2019, so you can see it for yourself.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.940
The most debated element of The Shining's
ending is the final shot, showing Jack in

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:14.880
the photo of the Overlook's 1921 Fourth of
July party, which could mean a few things.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:19.280
It could be that in death, Jack has been absorbed
into The Overlook's history, and now walks

00:01:19.289 --> 00:01:21.979
the haunted world where all eras seem to be
happening at once.

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:26.549
It could also be that the photo is the hotel's
way of showing that Jack's descent into madness

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:29.120
was the fulfilment of some kind of twisted
destiny.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.520
One particular interpretation has been adopted
by many of the film's hardcore fans.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:37.390
The Overlook's long series of caretaker murders
and the fact that it was built on an Indian

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:41.320
burial ground suggests that the hotel is a
place where history repeats itself.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.980
Many believe Jack was a reincarnated soul
who was always meant to be part of that cycle.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.600
He was destined to return to the hotel and
re-create its bloody past.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:53.640
According to the theory, this is what the
spectral Grady means during the conversation

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.280
in the red bathroom when he tells Jack,

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.300
"You are the caretaker.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.260
You’ve always been the caretaker."

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.300
If you watch The Shining all the way through,
get to that final shot of Jack in the photo,

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.620
and then rewatch it, you will notice something
that stands out about The Overlook.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:16.760
There are framed black and white photos chronicling
the hotel's history everywhere.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:21.400
Dozens of them, all in identical frames, all
covering the hotel's illustrious history.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:26.440
If Jack was a reincarnation of a past guest
or caretaker, and he was always in the photo,

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.780
how long will it be before someone notices
him there?

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:33.020
And if he's there, it stands to reason that
Grady would be there too, proving he was also

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.970
a victim of the cycle of reincarnation and
violence.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:40.420
If the fallout from the latest Overlook tragedy
goes on long enough, those photos will likely

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:44.560
mean something to someone, and that will set
off an entirely different kind of investigation.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:50.019
Early in The Shining when Dick Hallorann sits
down with Danny to explain to him what the

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.120
shining is, he tells the boy that he's not
alone.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:58.380
"But there are other folks, though mostly,
they don't know it, or don’t believe it."

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:03.480
Considering both Hallorann and Danny, two
of the rare souls with this psychic gift,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.120
find themselves in The Overlook, does this
mean that in the world of The Shining people

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:11.019
with the gift are drawn to places with powerful
supernatural presences, or are they able to

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.040
sense the spirits present wherever they are?

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:17.520
The film only scratches the surface, but as
Dick draws closer to his violent end, Danny's

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.060
journey is just beginning.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.620
"Boy, you read my thoughts!

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:22.780
You've got the Shinnin'!"

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.080
"You mean Shining."

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:24.760
"Shhhh!

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:25.860
You wanna get sued?"

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:31.300
The Shining is a film about cyclical violence,
and about how we can't escape the dark specter

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:32.120
of history.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:35.739
The Overlook is built on the sacred lands
of Native Americans who were slaughtered in

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.580
the name of expansion, and blood continues
to spill there.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:42.960
Jack Torrance's own history with violence
doesn't begin in The Overlook, but years before

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.080
when he hurt his son during a drunken binge.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:49.600
That's why when Wendy sees Danny injured she
immediately assumes it was Jack who hurt him.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:53.939
Just as The Overlook uses Jack to continue
its own bloody tradition, it traps him back

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:57.380
in his own past when drinking allows him to
cross the line into violence.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:00.500
But this time, the stakes are much higher
than a broken bone or two.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:01.980
"I’m not gonna hurt ya.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:04.700
I’m just gonna bash your brains in."

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:09.820
Like Jack, The Overlook has its own grisly
cycle, first the Native Americans killed for

00:04:09.829 --> 00:04:13.939
its creation, Grady murdering his family,
and now Jack attempting to slaughter his wife

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:14.460
and son.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.020
It's quite possible there were others we never
heard about.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.840
Fortunately, Wendy and Danny escape the hotel
relatively unscathed; at least physically.

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:25.500
Does this mean that, through their survival,
they've somehow broken the cycle?

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.480
Does this mean The Overlook's evil sway is
at least somewhat lessened?

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:31.140
The film doesn't make the answer clear, but
there is hope.

